From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel <daniel@matv.io>
Date: Tue, 02 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] awb: neutralize the residual green cast in the CPU non-CCM
 path

FAJITA CPU-COLOR BASELINE (2026-09-02). With the CCM re-parked
(imx376.yaml STATUS block: the CPU CCM STORE_PIXEL path costs ~3x the
flat-LUT path and dropped the ~5 MP viewfinder to ~5 fps, breaking AF),
the shipped rear look is a mild flat green-grey daylight cast + low
saturation. Saturation is UNREACHABLE on this path -- Adjust's
applySaturation() only mutates context.combinedMatrix, which the debayer
reads ONLY when ccmEnabled (if constexpr in STORE_PIXEL); the flat
non-CCM branch reads params.gains alone. And a diagonal CCM does NOT help:
ccmEnabled is a COMPILE-TIME template arg, so the CCM branch always does
the full 3x3 (3 CcmColumn lookups + 6 int adds + 3 gamma-clamp lookups)
per pixel whether or not the off-diagonals are zero -- a diagonal "CCM" is
exactly as expensive as the full matrix, not free. So the only genuinely
zero-per-pixel-cost color lever on the CPU path is the per-channel WB
gains that already feed the flat LUT.

The gray-world AWB pins gains.g = 1.0 and pulls R/B toward the scene
mean, which after black-subtraction (patches 12/13) settles the rendered
channel means around ~163/162/163 -- neutral-but-green-leaning (G leads R
by a few counts). This applies a tiny fixed trim to the post-gray-world
gains to lift R and B fractionally relative to G, pushing the neutral
point off green toward true grey. It is intentionally small (a couple of
percent) -- gray-world already does the heavy lifting; this only removes
the residual bias it leaves on this sensor. Tune kGreenCastTrim* by eye.

SCOPING / NO DOUBLE-CORRECTION: gated on !context.ccmEnabled, so it runs
ONLY on the CPU flat-LUT path shipped today. The moment the CCM is
re-listed (which also enables the GPU debayer's combinedMatrix path and
Adjust's saturation), this nudge becomes a no-op and the CCM owns color
-- there is no path where the trim and a matrix both apply. Even without
the gate the trim only shifts awb.gains (the single WB value every path
derives from), so it could never stack as a second correction on top of a
CCM; the gate just makes that guarantee unconditional and keeps the
calibrated CCM's white point untouched.
---
 src/ipa/simple/algorithms/awb.cpp | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

--- a/src/ipa/simple/algorithms/awb.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/simple/algorithms/awb.cpp
@@ -112,6 +112,27 @@
 		std::clamp(gains.b(), 1.15f, 2.60f),
 	} };
 
+	/*
+	 * fajita (IMX376), CPU non-CCM baseline: neutralize the residual
+	 * green-grey cast the gray-world AWB leaves on this sensor. gains.g is
+	 * pinned at 1.0, so lifting R and B by a small fixed factor shifts the
+	 * rendered neutral point off green toward true grey at ZERO extra
+	 * per-pixel cost (these gains feed the flat non-CCM debayer LUT
+	 * directly). Gated on !ccmEnabled: when the CCM/GPU path is enabled the
+	 * calibrated matrix owns colour and this trim must not perturb its
+	 * white point, so it becomes a no-op. A/B by setting both trims to 1.0
+	 * (identity = old behaviour); raise to push further off green.
+	 */
+	if (!context.ccmEnabled) {
+		constexpr float kGreenCastTrimR = 1.015f;
+		constexpr float kGreenCastTrimB = 1.015f;
+		gains = { {
+			std::clamp(gains.r() * kGreenCastTrimR, 1.10f, 2.70f),
+			1.0f,
+			std::clamp(gains.b() * kGreenCastTrimB, 1.15f, 2.60f),
+		} };
+	}
+
 	RGB<double> rgbGains{ { 1 / gains.r(), 1 / gains.g(), 1 / gains.b() } };
 	context.activeState.awb.temperatureK = estimateCCT(rgbGains);
 	metadata.set(controls::ColourTemperature, context.activeState.awb.temperatureK);
